2.text:
With a single tower the Reve / Frame-Stewart number is

* $T(3,5)=5$, $T(4,4)=9$, $T(6,4)=17$

and

$T(9,5)=\min_{k}\{2T(k,5)+T(9-k,4)\}=2T(3,5)+T(6,4)=2\cdot5+17=27$

is the $1\to1$ five peg diameter.  The start you quote is not a
single tower.  Write the pegs bottom $\to$ top

* $0:7\!-\!3\!-\!2$, $1:1$, $2:8\!-\!6$, $3:9\!-\!5\!-\!4$, $4:\varnothing $

The 9 can be freed in the forced four moves

* $4:3\to4$, $5:3\to2$, $4:4\to2$, $9:3\to4$

i.e.

$0:7\!-\!3\!-\!2,\;1:1,\;2:8\!-\!6\!-\!5\!-\!4,\;3:\varnothing,\;
4:9$

after which the 9 is fixed.  Up to symmetry the rest of the
packing can be done in the $16$ moves

$6:2\to3,\;5:2\to4,\;4:2\to3,\;8:2\to4,\;
2:0\to2,\;3:0\to4,\;7:0\to4,\;3:4\to0,\;
1:1\to2,\;4:1\to3,\;5:1\to4,\;4:3\to4,\;
3:0\to4,\;1:2\to0,\;2:2\to4,\;1:0\to4$

together with the four moves used to free the 9.  In the usual
numbering this is the $20$ move walk

$1\!:\!1\!\to\!0,\;4\!:\!3\!\to\!4,\;5\!:\!3\!\to\!2,\;4\!:\!4\!\to\!2,\;
9\!:\!3\!\to\!4,\;6\!:\!2\!\to\!3,\;8\!:\!2\!\to\!4,\;
2\!:\!0\!\to\!2,\;3\!:\!0\!\to\!4,\;7\!:\!0\!\to\!4,\;
3\!:\!4\!\to\!0,\;6\!:\!3\!\to\!4,\;1\!:\!1\!\to\!2,\;
4\!:\!1\!\to\!3,\;5\!:\!1\!\to\!4,\;4\!:\!3\!\to\!4,\;
3\!:\!0\!\to\!4,\;1\!:\!2\!\to\!0,\;2\!:\!2\!\to\!4,\;
1\!:\!0\!\to\!4$

which terminates in

$0=\varnothing,\;1=\varnothing,\;2=\varnothing,\;3=\varnothing,\;
4=9\!-\!8\!-\!7\!-\!6\!-\!5\!-\!4\!-\!3\!-\!2\!-\!1\,.$

Hence the distance of the quoted scattered start from the target
tower on $P_{4}$ is $20$ moves, $7$ moves less than the ordinary
$1\to1$ Frame-Stewart number $T(9,5)=27$.  The $27$ move number is
the conventional Reve optimum for a complete $9$ disc tower on five
pegs; the $20$ move realisation above is the optimum for the
particular initial distribution $P_{0}=7\,3\,2,\;P_{1}=1,\;P_{2}=8\,6,\;
P_{3}=9\,5\,4$.  Up to the usual $3$ move saving already present in
the start this is the $T(9,5)$ bound, the $21\!-\!23$ move
realisations being the same walk with an inessential extra
temporary move for the $1$ disc. ∎

